---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { allFacts } from '../lib/facts';

const count = Object.keys(allFacts).length;
const checked = Object.values(allFacts)
  .map((f) => f.fetchedAt)
  .sort()
  .at(-1);
---

<Layout
  title="About — PLAYHUNTER"
  description="Why a discovery site for paid Android games exists, how the facts stay honest, and what we will never do."
>
  <article class="shell wrap">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>The discovery layer Google Play never built</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose measure">
      <p>
        Go look at a PlayStation game's page on the open web. You'll find what the game <em>is</em>,
        why it's worth playing, its key features, a FAQ, real screenshots, a trailer — an actual
        argument, made with actual craft. Now look up a paid mobile game. You get a store listing:
        eight screenshots, four thousand characters of developer copy, and a price with no case
        attached to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That gap is why people believe there are no good games on phones. There are — some of the
        best games of the past decade live on Google Play for less than the price of lunch. What
        they've never had is someone making the argument for them.
      </p>
      <p>PLAYHUNTER makes the argument. Every page here answers the questions a listing won't:
        What is this game? Why is it worth real money upfront? What does it feel like at 1 a.m.?
        Who should skip it?</p>

      <h2>How the facts stay honest</h2>
      <p>
        Every hard number on this site — prices, ratings, download counts, IAP flags, update dates —
        is pulled programmatically from the game's live Google Play listing, not typed by hand.
        Currently tracking {count} games; facts last checked {checked}. If a game contains in-app
        purchases, we say so in the second row of the page, because pretending otherwise is how
        trust dies.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will never do</h2>
      <p>
        No scores out of ten. No sponsored placements. No affiliate skimming — Google Play doesn't
        even have an affiliate program, which makes this easy to promise. Every outbound link goes
        to the game's real Play listing and nowhere else. If a game stops being worth your money,
        its page changes or disappears.
      </p>

      <h2>Who's behind this</h2>
      <p>
        PLAYHUNTER is an independent games project. We also build our own mobile games — which is
        exactly why we care that paying for a good one stays a normal thing to do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <style>
    .wrap { padding-block: 4.5rem 2rem; }
    h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5.5vw, 3.4rem); margin-block: 0.75rem 2rem; max-width: 46rem; }
    .prose h2 {
      font-size: 1.4rem;
      margin-block: 2.25rem 0.75rem;
      color: var(--fg);
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
